    47	
    48	## SHA-256 tarball manifest — what actually exists
    49	
    50	`npm/SHA256SUMS.txt` is in the tree, and it is easy to read it as a shipped
    51	guarantee. It is not one:
    52	
    53	- It is written by **`scripts/install-npm.sh`**, a local build helper a
    54	  maintainer runs on a workstation. No workflow invokes it, and no workflow
    55	  appends to the file on tag push.
    56	- It therefore records whatever tarball was last built locally, which lags
    57	  the published package.
    58	- It does **not** travel inside the package: `SHA256SUMS.txt` is absent from
    59	  the `files:` array in `npm/package.json`, so it is not in the tarball a
    60	  consumer installs.
    61	
    62	Earlier revisions of this document published a consumer recipe that ran
    63	`sha256sum -c` over that manifest from inside the installed package. The
    64	recipe has been **removed, not annotated**: it could never run, for any
    65	version, because the file it reads is not in the package. A caveated
    66	impossible recipe is still an impossible recipe, and it is not reproduced
    67	here — a reader who finds a command in a doc will run it.
    68	
    69	What a consumer can actually verify is the provenance attestation — see
    70	§SLSA Level-2 provenance below. For the bash install path, the
    71	`install.sh.sha256` release asset is the checksum that exists; `SECURITY.md`
    72	§How to verify what you install states its scope and its limits.
    73	
    74	## Signing keys
    75	
    76	The Owner's public key is committed in-repo at `.claude/trust/owner.asc`.
    77	What it signs today is **release tags**. That gate lives outside this
    78	contract and is documented — with its exact scope — in `SECURITY.md`
    79	§How to verify what you install, which is its authority; this file does not
    80	restate it. Verify locally with
    81	`gpg --import .claude/trust/owner.asc && git tag --verify vX.Y.Z`.
    82	
    83	There is **no** separate npm signing key. Two distribution points were
    84	claimed here and neither exists: a fingerprint in `docs/rotation-log.md`
    85	(that log covers API-key rotation — its NPM entry records the retirement of
    86	`NPM_TOKEN` in favour of OIDC, and it publishes no key material), and a
    87	public key served under a `.well-known/` path (this project serves no such
    88	path at all). The detached-signature row above is `operator` for that reason:
    89	nothing signs a tarball today.
    90	
    91	## SLSA Level-2 provenance
    92	
    93	`npm publish --provenance` attaches a Sigstore-signed attestation declaring:
    94	- Source repository (git URL)
    95	- Commit SHA
    96	- Workflow file that built the tarball
    97	- Builder (GitHub Actions runner identity)
    98	
    99	Consumers inspect via one of two routes. `npm audit signatures` reads the
   100	dependency tree of the CURRENT project and takes no package-selecting
   101	argument (a trailing package name is ignored), so it reaches this package
   102	only when this package is a dependency of the project you run it in. On a
   103	global install it exits with `EAUDITGLOBAL`, whose message states that
   104	global packages are not supported.
   105	
   106	Route A — global install (`npm install -g`) or `npx`: open the
   107	"Provenance" panel on <https://www.npmjs.com/package/ceo-orchestration>
   108	and confirm the source repository shown there is
   109	`Canhada-Labs/ceo-orchestration`.
   110	
   111	Route B — audit locally, by making the package a dependency of a
   112	throwaway project:
   113	
   114	```bash
   115	mkdir /tmp/verify-ceo && cd /tmp/verify-ceo && npm init -y
   116	npm install ceo-orchestration
   117	npm audit signatures   # audits THIS project's tree, which now holds it
   118	```
   119	
   120	## Reproducible-build spec
   121	
   122	**Status: specification only — no workflow implements any part of it.**
   123	Inputs:
   124	- `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH = <VERSION tag creator-date, unix-epoch>`
   125	- Node 20.x (`npm-publish.yml` step "Setup Node 20")
   126	- No `npm install` for the bundle itself (zero runtime deps)
   127	
   128	Intended output: byte-identical tarball across any ubuntu-latest GitHub
   129	Actions runner with the same inputs. No workflow performs the rebuild or the
   130	comparison, and no workflow sets `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH` at all, so a deviation
   131	would go unnoticed.
   132	
   133	## CI verification (what the packlist gate does, and does not, prove)
   134	
   135	Both `npm-publish.yml` and `validate.yml` run a packlist gate over
   136	`npm pack --dry-run --json`, and its subject is the **file list** the tarball
   137	would contain (no tests, fixtures, eval corpora or plan material). `--dry-run`
   138	writes no archive, so the gate proves nothing about tarball **bytes** — no
   139	checksum, no signature, no reproducibility. Tarball hashing and signing are
   140	the un-automated controls above; provenance is the one byte-level attestation
   141	that ships.
   142	
   143	## Not yet automated (release-operator or out of scope)
   144	
   145	- **SHA-256 tarball manifest** — no workflow materialises the `.tgz`, so
   146	  none can hash it; `npm/SHA256SUMS.txt` is a local-build artefact and is not
   147	  shipped inside the package.
   148	- **GPG detached signature** would be a manual release-operator step (sign
   149	  the tarball locally; attach the `.asc` to the GitHub Release). It is not a
   150	  CI gate, there is no published npm signing key, and no release has shipped
   151	  one.
   152	- **Reproducible build** (`SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH`-pinned `npm pack`) is specified
   153	  above but not yet asserted byte-for-byte in CI.
   154	- **SLSA Level-3** (hermetic build + two-party review) is out of scope; the
   155	  shipped provenance is SLSA Level-2 (`--provenance`).
   156	
